WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.760 --> 00:00:06.000
In the Middle Ages, the most important&nbsp;
worship place in the City of London&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.000 --> 00:00:11.760
was Saint Paul’s Cathedral, a magnificent&nbsp;
Gothic structure whose mass dominated the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:11.760 --> 00:00:17.760
skyline of the medieval city even from&nbsp;
the opposite side of the Thames river.

00:00:17.760 --> 00:00:23.840
Saint Paul’s enormous nave, was longer&nbsp;
than that of any other cathedral in Europe,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.840 --> 00:00:28.960
and its legendary spire, taller&nbsp;
even than the Great Pyramid of Giza,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.960 --> 00:00:35.200
made the cathedral the second tallest&nbsp;
structure in the world at the time.

00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:42.080
Saint Paul’s Cathedral rose upon Ludgate Hill, the&nbsp;
highest point of Londinium, the old Roman city.

00:00:42.080 --> 00:00:47.120
Construction began in 1087 over the&nbsp;
ruins of an earlier small church.

00:00:47.120 --> 00:00:53.040
Saint Paul’s started as a Norman Romanesque&nbsp;
structure, with rounded arches and small windows,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.040 --> 00:00:58.800
but due to fires and problems within the&nbsp;
kingdom, the construction was long delayed.

00:00:58.800 --> 00:01:04.240
At the time, architecture was ever on the change,&nbsp;
and the builders didn’t trouble themselves to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:04.240 --> 00:01:10.480
follow the style in which a building had been&nbsp;
begun, but went on with whatever was in use then.

00:01:10.480 --> 00:01:15.840
By the 12th century, Gothic architecture had&nbsp;
become the standard for church building across&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:15.840 --> 00:01:20.160
Europe, and because churches often took&nbsp;
centuries to complete, most cathedrals in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:20.160 --> 00:01:27.360
England have some Romanesque elements as well as&nbsp;
some early Gothic and some late Gothic elements.

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:31.280
Old Saint Paul’s Cathedral was no&nbsp;
exception: it took so long to build&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.280 --> 00:01:39.840
that it was a very hybrid combination of&nbsp;
Norman Romanesque and Gothic architecture.

00:01:39.840 --> 00:01:45.040
The western half was built first, so&nbsp;
it retained its Romanesque appearance:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.040 --> 00:01:48.320
smaller windows, round arches, and a thicker,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:48.320 --> 00:01:55.120
more massive wall with a lesser need for flying&nbsp;
buttresses, which were evidently added later.

00:01:55.120 --> 00:02:01.280
And the eastern half was built later, thus you&nbsp;
can notice it looks more Gothic in its forms,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:06.000
with pointed arches in the windows and vaults.

00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:11.920
Also the Cathedral was heavily repaired and&nbsp;
modified over the centuries, so the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.920 --> 00:02:19.200
striking elements such as the exquisite rose&nbsp;
window at the east were in the Gothic style.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:26.160
The spire was a structure of timber covered with&nbsp;
lead, and it reached a height of 149 meters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:30.640
making Old Saint Paul’s the second&nbsp;
tallest building on Earth at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:30.640 --> 00:02:39.600
just behind Lincoln’s Cathedral, in central&nbsp;
England, whose spire reached 160 meters.

00:02:39.600 --> 00:02:44.960
In 1561, during a severe thunderstorm,&nbsp;
Saint Paul’s spire was struck by lightning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:44.960 --> 00:02:51.600
and caught fire, making the entire spire&nbsp;
collapse, and it was never rebuilt.

00:02:51.600 --> 00:02:56.000
The front facade had a distinctly&nbsp;
Romanesque character, featuring&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:03.440
solid masonry walls with small windows, and&nbsp;
deep, round archivolts framing the doors.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:09.200
The Great Cathedral of Saint Paul, glorious&nbsp;
as it was, the finest in England in its time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:09.200 --> 00:03:14.320
lasted on for nearly six centuries, but&nbsp;
after the Reformation, it began to decline.

00:03:14.320 --> 00:03:20.240
The English Reformation led to the destruction of&nbsp;
interior ornamentation such as altars, shrines,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:20.240 --> 00:03:24.800
chapels, and crypts, as well as several&nbsp;
religious buildings in the churchyard,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:24.800 --> 00:03:30.560
which were seized by the crown and sold&nbsp;
as shops to protestant booksellers.

00:03:30.560 --> 00:03:34.640
By the end of the sixteenth century,&nbsp;
the cathedral had lost its spire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:34.640 --> 00:03:41.120
its walls were falling into disrepair,&nbsp;
and much of its heritage had been lost.

00:03:43.360 --> 00:03:49.520
The great nave, once a place of worship, became&nbsp;
known as Paul’s Walk, and had become a market,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.520 --> 00:03:55.040
and the place in London where you’d go to hear&nbsp;
the latest news and to do business, but also a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.040 --> 00:04:01.120
place to pick up gossip and even prostitutes,&nbsp;
a place infested by beggars and thieves.

00:04:01.120 --> 00:04:07.360
In 1621, King James appointed Inigo Jones,&nbsp;
the first Renaissance architect in England,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:07.360 --> 00:04:11.120
to repair the structure of the&nbsp;
Cathedral, and to rebuild some parts.

00:04:11.120 --> 00:04:17.040
In the 1630s Jones added a&nbsp;
Classical porch to the front.

00:04:17.040 --> 00:04:21.760
In the early 1660s Christopher Wren,&nbsp;
a mathematician and astronomer,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:21.760 --> 00:04:24.720
with some experience as an&nbsp;
architect, was appointed to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:29.680
restore the building matching the style&nbsp;
of Inigo Jones’s additions in the 1630s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:29.680 --> 00:04:36.080
and he proposed to demolish part of the central&nbsp;
tower, and replace it with a Renaissance dome.

00:04:36.080 --> 00:04:40.000
Even though his design was approved&nbsp;
and restoration works started,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:40.000 --> 00:04:47.920
Saint Paul’s Cathedral was destroyed&nbsp;
in the Great Fire of London in 1666.

00:04:47.920 --> 00:04:57.040
The vaults of most Gothic cathedrals are usually&nbsp;
made of stone, but the vaults of the old Saint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.040 --> 00:05:01.760
Paul’s Cathedral were made of wood, and&nbsp;
therefore much more vulnerable to fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:01.760 --> 00:05:08.160
which affected the ultimate fate of the&nbsp;
building, reducing the structure to ruins.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:12.640
Christopher Wren was appointed to oversee&nbsp;
the rebuilding of London after the fire,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:12.640 --> 00:05:16.240
thanks to the ingenious plan that he&nbsp;
proposed for the reconstruction of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.240 --> 00:05:20.560
the city, and he designed over fifty&nbsp;
parish churches to be reconstructed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.560 --> 00:05:26.240
all with a unique design that explored&nbsp;
new ideas of light, space, and order.

00:05:27.600 --> 00:05:29.600
Yet his greatest achievement,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:37.360
his architectural masterpiece would&nbsp;
be the new Saint Paul’s Cathedral.

00:05:37.360 --> 00:05:42.240
The destruction of the old cathedral was&nbsp;
not total, and at first Wren began a partial&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:42.240 --> 00:05:48.640
rebuilding of it, but by 1668, it became clear&nbsp;
that a complete reconstruction was necessary,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:48.640 --> 00:05:51.840
so he proposed his first&nbsp;
design for the new cathedral:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.840 --> 00:05:56.560
A centralized plan in the shape of a Greek&nbsp;
cross, whose arms were connected by a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.560 --> 00:06:01.360
series of curved quadrants instead of the&nbsp;
traditional solution of right angles found&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.360 --> 00:06:06.480
in Bramante’s or Michelangelo’s original&nbsp;
plans for Saint Peter’s Basilica in Rome.

00:06:06.480 --> 00:06:09.760
Wren incorporated a round choir on the east side,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.760 --> 00:06:14.960
and a large domed crossing similar&nbsp;
to that of Saint Peter’s Basilica.

00:06:14.960 --> 00:06:17.920
When making his famous&nbsp;
wooden model for the church,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:17.920 --> 00:06:23.920
Wren added a projecting western vestibule&nbsp;
to his design, capped by a smaller dome.

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:28.320
As you can see from Wren’s model and later&nbsp;
drawings, this design takes much from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:28.320 --> 00:06:36.880
Michelangelo’s architecture: his colossal order on&nbsp;
the exterior pilasters, his Pantheon-like portico,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:36.880 --> 00:06:42.480
and the huge dome as an urban element that’s&nbsp;
meant to be seen from anywhere in the city.

00:06:43.200 --> 00:06:47.760
However, this design was ultimately&nbsp;
dismissed by the clergy because it&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:52.320
seemed too different from the rest of&nbsp;
English cathedrals and too similar to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:52.320 --> 00:06:59.120
the Roman Catholic Churches that were being&nbsp;
built in Rome at the time, such as Saint Peter.

00:06:59.120 --> 00:07:06.000
The clergy desired a more traditional basilical&nbsp;
plan with a long nave, transepts and choir,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:10.800
more similar to the plan of the old&nbsp;
cathedral that had burned in 1666.

00:07:10.800 --> 00:07:15.680
Although now protestants, they were very used&nbsp;
to the architectural program of the medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.680 --> 00:07:21.440
Catholic type of plan, as they had been in&nbsp;
use for centuries in England, and they had a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:21.440 --> 00:07:26.800
certain rejection for the baroque character that&nbsp;
the Catholics were defining in the continent.

00:07:26.800 --> 00:07:32.720
So Christopher Wren redesigned the entire&nbsp;
project, returning to a Latin cross plan.

00:07:32.720 --> 00:07:38.720
He added a great octagonal space in the&nbsp;
crossing and a dome topped by a spire.

00:07:39.680 --> 00:07:46.160
It’s kind of trying to hold these Italian and&nbsp;
French baroque impulses expressed so strongly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.160 --> 00:07:51.760
in his previous design, and to make something&nbsp;
that uses a classical language with columns&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:51.760 --> 00:07:58.080
and round arches, but still reprises the Gothic&nbsp;
image of Old Saint Paul’s Cathedral in its form.

00:07:58.080 --> 00:08:03.760
This was the design that was approved,&nbsp;
and its construction began in 1675,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:03.760 --> 00:08:09.280
however, Wren as the architect-in-chief of the&nbsp;
construction, was given complete liberty to make&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.280 --> 00:08:14.720
any changes and variations to his own design as&nbsp;
he should see proper during the construction.

00:08:14.720 --> 00:08:16.880
So when we see the finished cathedral,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:16.880 --> 00:08:22.960
we find a completely different building&nbsp;
to the one that was originally approved.

00:08:22.960 --> 00:08:28.640
The final result is a refined building, with&nbsp;
perfect proportions, where you can see a clear&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:28.640 --> 00:08:33.040
influence of the Renaissance masters&nbsp;
of Italy and France in every element.

00:08:34.640 --> 00:08:39.200
Christopher Wren, the architect, was born&nbsp;
at a time when baroque architecture was at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.200 --> 00:08:44.960
its highest point of development in cities&nbsp;
like Rome or like Paris, and you can really&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.960 --> 00:08:51.440
tell that he visited those cities, and he had&nbsp;
seen the architecture of masters like Bernini,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:51.440 --> 00:08:56.560
like Maderno, especially when you&nbsp;
look at details on the facade and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:56.560 --> 00:09:03.600
on the elevations of this cathedral as well&nbsp;
as the design of the dome and the towers.

00:09:03.600 --> 00:09:08.560
Here are a few examples of direct influence&nbsp;
that Wren drew from other buildings:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.560 --> 00:09:12.640
The porticoes on the north and&nbsp;
south sides are enlarged copies of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:12.640 --> 00:09:20.080
the facade of Santa Maria della Pace&nbsp;
in Rome made by Pietro da Cortona.

00:09:20.080 --> 00:09:25.120
The double colonnade on the front portico,&nbsp;
as well as the double pilasters on the sides&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.120 --> 00:09:30.800
are taken from the facade of the&nbsp;
Louvre in Paris by Claude Perrault.

00:09:30.800 --> 00:09:33.440
Those lateral windows of the front facade,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.440 --> 00:09:38.720
recessed in a concave perspective to show the&nbsp;
thickness of the wall for visual strength, are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.720 --> 00:09:46.880
identical to the top windows found in the Palazzo&nbsp;
Barberini in Rome, made by Francesco Borromini.

00:09:46.880 --> 00:09:51.120
And the towers, are also reminiscent&nbsp;
of the architecture of Borromini,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.120 --> 00:09:56.880
when you compare them to the towers of the&nbsp;
church of Sant’Agnese in Piazza Navona, in Rome:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:56.880 --> 00:09:59.520
you can see that classic Borromini curve with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:59.520 --> 00:10:11.920
columns rotated 45 degrees, and they both&nbsp;
have a clock, and a bell shape at the top.

00:10:11.920 --> 00:10:14.000
All these elements make the new Saint Paul’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:14.000 --> 00:10:18.320
Cathedral look like a building from&nbsp;
the Renaissance and Baroque period,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:18.320 --> 00:10:24.480
however if you focus more on the plan of the&nbsp;
building, on the structure, and on its massing,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.480 --> 00:10:29.200
you can see the clear influence from&nbsp;
Gothic medieval cathedrals from England.

00:10:29.200 --> 00:10:33.040
Saint Paul’s has an extremely&nbsp;
long nave compared to its width,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:33.040 --> 00:10:38.000
something typical of the English Gothic,&nbsp;
and the huge crossing space with a dome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:38.000 --> 00:10:42.160
replaces the massive central tower&nbsp;
in English Gothic cathedrals.

00:10:42.160 --> 00:10:45.600
Wren is trying to make a refined&nbsp;
and academic building with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:45.600 --> 00:10:49.600
classical character that follows the&nbsp;
principles of Renaissance architecture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.600 --> 00:10:53.840
but he as a mathematician finds many&nbsp;
solutions that can’t coexist with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:53.840 --> 00:10:59.040
rules of Classical architecture, so he has&nbsp;
to disguise some of the structural elements.

00:10:59.040 --> 00:11:04.240
You can see that above the aisle roofs&nbsp;
there are these flying buttresses which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.240 --> 00:11:07.760
are Gothic elements, present&nbsp;
in most Gothic cathedrals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:13.600
so he hides these Gothic elements by raising&nbsp;
the aisle walls creating these screens that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:13.600 --> 00:11:22.480
cover them, and thus giving the exterior a good&nbsp;
proportional height in relation to the dome.

00:11:22.480 --> 00:11:25.680
These screen walls also act&nbsp;
as counterweight to the flying&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:25.680 --> 00:11:31.680
buttresses to redirect to the ground&nbsp;
the thrust received by the nave vault,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:31.680 --> 00:11:36.160
the same function that the pinnacles&nbsp;
have in Gothic architecture.

00:11:39.440 --> 00:11:44.160
To support the dome, Wren combines&nbsp;
different structural principles:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:48.240
Instead of just supporting a huge dome&nbsp;
over four massive piers like in Saint&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.240 --> 00:11:56.640
Peter’s and other Italian churches, he uses a&nbsp;
Gothic example as inspiration, Ely Cathedral.

00:11:56.640 --> 00:12:02.400
Among the medieval cathedrals in England, Ely&nbsp;
Cathedral is very special, because it has a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:02.400 --> 00:12:10.080
unique octagonal crossing with a great wooden&nbsp;
vault reaching to a central octagonal lantern,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.080 --> 00:12:15.200
and what could look like four great piers&nbsp;
in plan, such as those from Saint Peter’s,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.200 --> 00:12:20.000
seems like they are dematerialized&nbsp;
into eight much lighter piers.

00:12:20.880 --> 00:12:25.200
So Wren takes this example and applies&nbsp;
it to the dome of Saint Paul’s.

00:12:25.200 --> 00:12:30.160
You have a huge crossing space&nbsp;
with eight thin and delicate piers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:30.160 --> 00:12:36.160
each reaching a small triangular pendentive&nbsp;
that holds the drum of the great dome.

00:12:36.160 --> 00:12:41.920
And because these piers are so thin, you don’t&nbsp;
really read them as piers but as columns,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.920 --> 00:12:47.360
and the structure seems very dematerialized,&nbsp;
and delicate, you get this sense of lightness,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.360 --> 00:12:53.440
it’s like Wren is interested&nbsp;
in the absence of material.

00:12:53.440 --> 00:12:58.560
For the actual dome, Wren followed at a certain&nbsp;
extent the principles of Michelangelo’s cupola&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:58.560 --> 00:13:04.880
in Rome, but he especially studied&nbsp;
the dome of Les Invalides in Paris,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:04.880 --> 00:13:10.160
which employs a triple-dome system:&nbsp;
the innermost dome, made of masonry,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:10.160 --> 00:13:16.160
has a great opening at the top through which&nbsp;
your view vanishes to the top of the middle dome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.160 --> 00:13:23.040
decorated with a fresco, giving&nbsp;
you a three-dimensional effect.

00:13:23.040 --> 00:13:27.920
This middle dome supports the wooden&nbsp;
structure of a third, outer dome.

00:13:27.920 --> 00:13:32.160
This strategy creates two scales: an&nbsp;
interior scale that keeps the right&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:32.160 --> 00:13:36.720
human proportions inside the building,&nbsp;
and an exterior scale that turns the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:36.720 --> 00:13:42.000
massive-looking dome into an urban&nbsp;
element visible from the distance.

00:13:42.000 --> 00:13:46.800
Since Christopher Wren needed to create a&nbsp;
dome tall enough to dominate London’s skyline,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:46.800 --> 00:13:51.040
yet still maintain proper proportion&nbsp;
within the interior of the building,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:51.040 --> 00:13:54.800
he adopted the triple-dome&nbsp;
strategy of Les Invalides.

00:13:54.800 --> 00:13:58.640
The inner dome is built of masonry&nbsp;
with a small opening at the top that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:58.640 --> 00:14:01.920
allows your view to vanish right&nbsp;
to the top of the middle dome.

00:14:01.920 --> 00:14:05.520
The middle dome is more of a&nbsp;
brick cone that supports both,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:10.240
the stone lantern and the wooden&nbsp;
structure of the outer dome.

00:14:10.240 --> 00:14:16.960
If you climb up inside, you can walk through the&nbsp;
space between the brick cone, and the outer dome,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:16.960 --> 00:14:22.400
where the timber framing and beams that support&nbsp;
the exterior shell rest on the brick structure,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:22.400 --> 00:14:27.440
along with thirty-two radiating counter-forts.

00:14:27.440 --> 00:14:31.920
If you look at the section of the building,&nbsp;
you can notice that the drum of the cupola&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.920 --> 00:14:38.160
is not vertical, it tapers slightly toward the&nbsp;
top, and the brick cone is not exactly a cone,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.160 --> 00:14:45.520
but along with the tapering drum, forms a softly&nbsp;
curved profile that isn’t exactly spherical.

00:14:45.520 --> 00:14:49.200
The hidden flying buttresses&nbsp;
also have this soft curve.

00:14:49.200 --> 00:14:53.920
Christopher Wren was a mathematician, and&nbsp;
along with his colleague Robert Hooke,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:53.920 --> 00:14:59.040
another illustrious mathematician of the&nbsp;
time, developed the catenary principle.

00:14:59.040 --> 00:15:03.520
The word “catenary” comes from the&nbsp;
Latin “catena” which means chain,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:03.520 --> 00:15:09.120
and the principle consists in the idea that the&nbsp;
shape a chain naturally takes when hanging under&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:09.120 --> 00:15:16.720
its own weight—the catenary curve—is also the most&nbsp;
stable form for an arch or dome when inverted.

00:15:16.720 --> 00:15:24.160
In other words, the curve that hangs in tension&nbsp;
becomes the perfect curve to stand in compression.

00:15:24.160 --> 00:15:26.480
And this is the structural principle that Wren&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.480 --> 00:15:30.320
used for the middle dome&nbsp;
and the flying buttresses.

00:15:30.320 --> 00:15:36.640
He kind of anticipated Antoni Gaudí by a couple&nbsp;
hundred years, but Wren hid these details behind&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:36.640 --> 00:15:42.640
the classical language of architecture, while&nbsp;
Gaudí created a language from these structural&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:42.640 --> 00:15:50.480
principles by expressing the true structure&nbsp;
instead of concealing it behind perfect geometry.

00:15:52.160 --> 00:15:56.560
The form of the outer dome was inspired&nbsp;
by the architecture of Donato Bramante,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:56.560 --> 00:16:00.720
one of the most respected Italian&nbsp;
architects from the High Renaissance.

00:16:00.720 --> 00:16:05.920
Wren especially took inspiration from&nbsp;
Bramante’s Tempietto, considered a perfect&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:05.920 --> 00:16:19.520
gem of the Renaissance because of its proportions&nbsp;
replicating those of ancient Roman round temples.

00:16:19.520 --> 00:16:24.240
The colonnade on the drum is formed&nbsp;
of three-quarter columns attached&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:24.240 --> 00:16:30.320
to radiating buttress walls, having every&nbsp;
fourth intercolumniation filled in solid,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:30.320 --> 00:16:33.920
giving an appearance of strength and solidity.

00:16:33.920 --> 00:16:41.120
Behind the balustrade above the columns there&nbsp;
is a gallery from where the dome is resting.

00:16:41.120 --> 00:16:46.000
At the summit of the dome, there are&nbsp;
eight openings to light the inner domes.

00:16:46.800 --> 00:16:55.040
The total height of the dome is 365 feet, a number&nbsp;
that was symbolic for Wren as an astronomer.

00:16:55.040 --> 00:16:58.880
At the meeting of the nave and&nbsp;
transepts there are projecting masses,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:58.880 --> 00:17:05.760
which express the support of the&nbsp;
dome from the ground upwards.

00:17:05.760 --> 00:17:10.240
This dome is certainly one of&nbsp;
the finest domes in Europe.

00:17:11.440 --> 00:17:17.600
To reach the top, you must first ascend the round&nbsp;
staircase located in the south side of the nave,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.600 --> 00:17:22.000
eventually emerging onto the gallery&nbsp;
that encircles the base of the drum.

00:17:22.000 --> 00:17:25.680
From here you can appreciate the&nbsp;
spectacular expanse of the nave&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:25.680 --> 00:17:28.960
and crossing, and especially&nbsp;
the geometric pattern of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:28.960 --> 00:17:39.040
pavement revealed with a clarity that’s&nbsp;
impossible to perceive from ground level.

00:17:39.040 --> 00:17:42.720
From there, the ascent continues&nbsp;
within the space between the brick&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.720 --> 00:17:48.000
cone and the outer dome where the&nbsp;
internal structure is fully exposed.

00:17:48.000 --> 00:17:56.720
The stairs grow narrower as&nbsp;
you approach the lantern.

00:17:56.720 --> 00:18:02.960
Once you reach the lantern there are just&nbsp;
a few stairs left to reach the summit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:08.080
and there is a small window there that allows&nbsp;
you to look straight down into the crossing.

00:18:11.440 --> 00:18:20.000
From the top of the dome, you can see the entire&nbsp;
city of London, the Thames with its bridges,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:20.000 --> 00:18:26.080
the modern skyline beyond, and&nbsp;
in the distance, Westminster.

00:18:26.080 --> 00:18:30.400
This view must have been overwhelming&nbsp;
at the time the cathedral was completed,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:30.400 --> 00:18:34.240
thanks to the skyline filled&nbsp;
with spires and monuments.

00:18:34.240 --> 00:18:39.600
It remained so until the late twentieth century,&nbsp;
for no building in the city rose higher than&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:39.600 --> 00:18:47.680
Saint Paul’s Cathedral, but London’s harmonious&nbsp;
skyline was eventually lost to modern skyscrapers.

00:18:47.680 --> 00:18:52.000
The interior of Saint Paul’s is comprised&nbsp;
by a long nave reminiscent of medieval&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:58.000
cathedrals in its volume, but expressed in&nbsp;
a classical language through its decoration.

00:18:58.880 --> 00:19:04.400
The piers are ornamented with Corinthian&nbsp;
pilasters supporting an entablature and attic,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:04.400 --> 00:19:08.640
above which are formed flat saucer&nbsp;
domes inspired by those in the church&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:08.640 --> 00:19:12.960
of Val-de-Grâce in Paris, which Wren had visited.

00:19:12.960 --> 00:19:15.600
Upon entering, you’ll notice&nbsp;
that most of the light inside&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.600 --> 00:19:20.000
the cathedral is admitted through the&nbsp;
clerestory at the top of the nave.

00:19:20.000 --> 00:19:22.560
These clerestory windows are invisible from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:22.560 --> 00:19:26.720
outside of the building because&nbsp;
they’re hidden by the screen wall.

00:19:29.360 --> 00:19:36.080
The vaults aren’t particularly tall&nbsp;
compared to other European cathedrals.

00:19:36.080 --> 00:19:41.360
Lower vaults is something inherited&nbsp;
from English Gothic architecture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:41.360 --> 00:19:46.640
and this makes the dome appear immense,&nbsp;
as it feels much closer to you than other&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:46.640 --> 00:19:51.760
great domes in Europe such as that&nbsp;
of Saint Peter’s at the Vatican.

00:19:51.760 --> 00:19:57.440
Behind the altar there is a three-bay choir&nbsp;
with spectacular wood carving by the sculptor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:57.440 --> 00:20:05.440
Grinling Gibbons, who was also responsible&nbsp;
for all the stone sculpture in the cathedral.

00:20:05.440 --> 00:20:08.400
Before entering the choir&nbsp;
you encounter exceptional&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:08.400 --> 00:20:20.480
wrought-iron screens crafted by&nbsp;
the French blacksmith Jean Tijou.

00:20:20.480 --> 00:20:25.600
And even here, inside the building you see all&nbsp;
these elements borrowed from Bernini and from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:25.600 --> 00:20:31.360
other baroque masters from Italy such as these&nbsp;
hexagonal patterns on the window coffering,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:31.360 --> 00:20:46.080
or these Palladian arches in the&nbsp;
vestibule leading to lateral chapels.

00:20:46.080 --> 00:21:03.307
In the crypt lies the tomb of Christopher Wren,&nbsp;
with a Latin inscription above that reads:

00:21:03.307 --> 00:21:03.396
“subtus conditur huius ecclesiae et urbis&nbsp;
conditor Christophorus Wren, qui vixit annos&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:03.396 --> 00:21:07.680
ultra nonaginta, non sibi sed bono publico.&nbsp;
Lector, si monumentum requiris, circumspice.”

00:21:07.680 --> 00:21:12.080
Below is buried the founder of this&nbsp;
church and city, Christopher Wren,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:12.080 --> 00:21:17.280
who lived over ninety years, not for&nbsp;
himself, but for the public good.

00:21:17.280 --> 00:21:21.600
Reader, if you seek a&nbsp;
monument of him, look around.

00:21:22.640 --> 00:21:28.720
Christopher Wren lived to see the cathedral&nbsp;
completed in 1710, something extremely rare&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:28.720 --> 00:21:35.040
in terms of great European churches, which&nbsp;
often took several generations to be built.

00:21:35.040 --> 00:21:37.920
Wren was one of the few architects to oversee the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:37.920 --> 00:21:44.000
entire construction process&nbsp;
and see his work completed.

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:51.200
That’s why when you see Saint Paul’s Cathedral,&nbsp;
it seems to harmonize in a special way:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.200 --> 00:21:54.720
unlike Saint Peter in Rome which&nbsp;
was built by several architects,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:54.720 --> 00:21:58.720
Saint Paul’s dome seems to be&nbsp;
much more a part of the building.

00:21:58.720 --> 00:22:01.680
Its grouping with the two fine symmetrical towers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:01.680 --> 00:22:05.280
gives the parts and the whole&nbsp;
a very cohesive harmony.

00:22:05.280 --> 00:22:10.800
You can really see how Wren is a great&nbsp;
a synthesizer of the past — absorbing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:10.800 --> 00:22:15.360
English Gothic mass and lightness,&nbsp;
French engineering and elegance,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:15.360 --> 00:22:23.520
and Italian classicism and baroque&nbsp;
into something uniquely English.

00:22:23.520 --> 00:22:30.720
The finished building ranks amongst the finest&nbsp;
Renaissance cathedrals in Europe, and its dome&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:30.720 --> 00:22:36.800
design defined a model that would be later adopted&nbsp;
by the French in their Pantheon, which was then&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:36.800 --> 00:22:46.400
copied by the Russians in Saint Isaac’s Cathedral,&nbsp;
and then by the Americans in their state Capitols.

00:22:46.400 --> 00:22:51.600
Yet even with such a legacy, none of&nbsp;
them quite match the serene balance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.600 --> 00:23:11.680
and luminous grandeur of Saint Paul’s itself.

00:23:18.080 --> 00:23:21.600
I've made an extended version of this&nbsp;
video where I go into more detail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:21.600 --> 00:23:25.680
on the old Saint Paul's Cathedral&nbsp;
as well as a new one, and explore&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:25.680 --> 00:23:29.840
other places from where Christopher&nbsp;
Wren took inspiration for his work.

00:23:29.840 --> 00:23:34.640
This longer video is available&nbsp;
if you join as a channel member.

00:23:34.640 --> 00:23:37.680
Thank you so much for watching.&nbsp;
I hope you learned and enjoyed.

00:23:37.680 --> 00:23:42.000
Please like this video, subscribe to my channel,&nbsp;
and I'll see you very soon in another episode.

00:23:42.000 --> 00:23:42.880
Goodbye!

